**The Erdős–Rado sunflower conjecture** (1960) states:

> There exists a constant $c > 0$ such that any family of more than $c^k$ sets, each of size at most $k$, contains a sunflower with 3 petals (i.e., three sets $A, B, C$ such that $A \cap B = A \cap C = B \cap C$).

This is a famous **open problem** in combinatorics. The best known upper bound is quasi-polynomial: $f(k) \leq 2^{O(k \log k)}$, established by Alweiss, Lovett, Wu, and Zhang (2019), with subsequent refinements by Rao and others. The conjectured exponential bound $f(k) = O(c^k)$ has **not been proven**.

Since this is an open conjecture, no complete proof is known. I cannot produce a proof of an unsolved problem.

